<header><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">Gojo · construction &amp; use</div><h1>One drawing.<br>Several kinds of joint.</h1><p class="lede">A bend, an attachment and a switch can leave the same mark. This atlas asks what the connected encoding preserves—and what a picture alone cannot tell us.</p><nav class="nav" aria-label="Study sections"><a href="#glyphs">1 · Glyph constructions</a><a href="#machines">2 · Joint machines</a><a href="#contexts">3 · Ground &amp; use</a><a href="#encoding">4 · The encoding</a><a href="README.md">Markdown record</a><a href="results.json">Recorded data</a></nav></div></header>
<main class="wrap"><div class="intro"><article><strong>What is connected?</strong><p>Actual shared sites determine topology. Two lines can cross on screen without being joined.</p></article><article><strong>How was it made?</strong><p>Ordered section visits preserve a continuous stroke, a lift, a return or an attachment.</p></article><article><strong>What can it do?</strong><p>Explicit route laws determine later flow. Drawing direction alone does not decide that.</p></article></div>
<p class="note">School-print baselines have been checked against the supplied teaching cards and the linked guides. Conventions differ; the chosen order is stated. Variants distinguish other teaching conventions from authored construction experiments. The native construction read uses earlier drawing as evidence, including an earlier part of the current stroke, rather than guessing history from the final outline.</p>
<section class="section" id="glyphs"><div class="section-header"><div><div class="kicker">01 / The artifact and its history</div><h2>Capital letters and digits</h2></div><div class="section-count" id="corpus-count"></div></div><p>Choose a glyph, then select a stroke to see where it starts, where it stops, and which earlier landmark guides it. A continuous corner differs from returning to a point and attaching another stroke.</p>
